# Scientific objective

Determine, by independent quantum-chemical calculation, whether the three supplied species—neutral singlet `(Et2N)3P`, neutral singlet `(Et2N)3P-PO`, and neutral singlet `(Et2N)3P-PA`—show the phosphonium/oxygen and phosphonium/chain-end interactions relevant to the paper's proposed mechanism. Report optimized structures, stationary-point validation, O···P/O···αH/O···βH distances where the named atoms exist, ADCH charge comparisons, and an interpretable MEP analysis.

# Author-provided scientific guidance

In reproduction mode, the authors' qualitative hypothesis is that an onium center can activate an epoxide and stabilize an alkoxide-like chain end through noncovalent contacts; test that hypothesis without assuming any numerical result.

# Public inputs and scientific boundaries

The files `data/inputs/Et2N3P.xyz`, `Et2N3P_PO.xyz`, and `Et2N3P_PA.xyz` are atom-indexed starting structures in Å for neutral singlet `(Et2N)3P`, `(Et2N)3P-PO`, and `(Et2N)3P-PA`. PO is the ring-opened propylene-oxide adduct; PA is the phthalic-anhydride-derived acylphosphonium/carboxylate zwitterion. The PA identity is `CCN(CC)[P+](C(=O)c1ccccc1C(=O)[O-])(N(CC)CC)N(CC)CC`, formula C20H34N3O3P. All three XYZ files are independent topology-only, unoptimized embeddings. The companion *_identity.json files give the complete mapped graphs; atom-map IDs equal the one-based XYZ rows. No author endpoint fragment, optimized contact distance or target charge was used to build these starters. The complete free-base and PO files contain 46 and 56 atoms, respectively. No additional species, counterion, solvent or polymer is part of the model.

For these N-ethyl substituents, αH denotes an N–CH2 hydrogen and βH an N–CH2–CH3 terminal hydrogen; the structure has P–N–C connectivity, not a direct P–C bond to an ethyl group. Derive and report this atom mapping from the inputs. Measure O···P, O···αH and O···βH contacts for the PO chain-end oxygen and the PA carboxylate group, retaining explicit oxygen and hydrogen indices. Report P and N-ethyl hydrogen ADCH charges, and analyze a signed electrostatic-potential surface. Distinguish electrostatic potential from electron density. All systems are isolated ground-state molecules. The paper and SI are unavailable during evaluation; no general web or literature search is allowed. Select and disclose software, functional, basis, dispersion, convergence and analysis settings. Do not infer targets from the supplied coordinates.

For both PO and PA, the primary contact oxygen O* is input atom-map ID 49 and phosphorus is ID 46. In PO, O* is the ring-opened chain-end oxygen; in PA, it is the carboxylate oxygen drawn as C48=O49 in the supplied mapped graph, not the other carboxylate oxygen O50 or the acyl oxygen O51. This identifies an observable; it does not constrain optimized distances, bond orders or computed charges. Preserve the input-to-output atom mapping through optimization and any coordinate renumbering; a chemically equivalent relabeling must still track the designated input atom, not exchange oxygens to improve the result.

On each species' own optimized geometry, set `contact_summary.<PO|PA>.O_P_A` to d(O*,P), `O_alphaH_A` to the minimum d(O*,H) over all N-ethyl αH atoms, and `O_betaH_A` to the minimum over all N-ethyl βH atoms. Use the same O* for all three fields: do not average contacts or minimize over different oxygens. Derive the hydrogen sets from the mapped chemical graph, not from distances in the unoptimized starter. For an exactly tied minimum, use the smallest input atom-map ID for the primary H and report the tie. Retain the selected pairs and distances in `species[].distances`, and explain any renumbering in `method.mapping_notes`. Keep the candidate O/H contact table (including the other PA carboxylate oxygen O50) as auxiliary evidence in the results or a referenced supporting file, not as a replacement for these three primary values. Report all mapped N-ethyl H ADCH charges; the highlighted contact-H charges must belong to the same H atoms selected by these distance rules. The free species has no oxygen and therefore no O/H contact selection. No private source-selected H label or desired numerical value is needed.

# Required scientific validation/investigation

Independently optimize all three supplied structures and perform a frequency or equivalent stationary-point check. Scientific completion requires all three structures optimized and validated as minima and all requested properties. If a required calculation fails, identify the species, cause, attempted remedy and unavailable observables; this records partial work, not a completed result. Measure each requested contact using explicit atom indices and preserve those indices in the report. Extract charges from the same validated wavefunction used for the charge analysis; do not mix atom order between structures. Produce an MEP visualization or numerical surface description and explain whether it supports, weakens, or does not resolve the qualitative hypothesis.

Use the explicit mapped graphs to establish P, each oxygen and every N-ethyl alpha/beta hydrogen; do not infer bond connectivity or charge solely from starter distances. The free, PO and PA systems contain 46, 56 and 61 atoms. All are net-neutral singlets; the PO and PA graphs explicitly retain the phosphonium/oxygen formal-charge separation. Relax the unoptimized geometry before interpreting distances or charges.

# Deliverables

Submit `report/results.json` following `submission_schema.json`, plus `report/structures/` files and `report/figures/` or numerical MEP data. The JSON must include method provenance, per-species status, frequency validation, atom-indexed distances and charges, MEP observations, a final mechanistic conclusion. Every successful or bounded-failure branch must identify what was actually computed; invented values are not acceptable.

Scientific completion requires all requested primary observables, the specified validation evidence and the resulting scientific comparison. A partial or failed calculation may be submitted with its actual results and diagnostics, but does not satisfy an uncomputed scientific result.

Additional starting structures or investigations may be used to obtain the required results. Report auxiliary results separately; they do not replace the primary observables or their specified definitions. Optional analyses may be omitted without explanation or penalty.
